WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.560
A gruesome scene that caught everyone&nbsp;
off guard. A darker Riddler than we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:09.440
ever seen. What scene went too far? Keep&nbsp;
watching. Spoilers for "The Batman" ahead!

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:14.880
A new Batman movie has burst forth from the night,&nbsp;
and audiences have been loving the new iteration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:18.880
of the iconic character. Robert Pattinson&nbsp;
perfectly fits the mold of both Bruce Wayne&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:23.600
and the Caped Crusader. And of course, no Batman&nbsp;
is complete without a sinister villain to fight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:28.080
Batman goes up against the likes of Penguin&nbsp;
and Carmine Falcone in the film, but the main&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:32.960
baddie that overshadows them all is Riddler.
Batman is known just as much for his intellect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:37.120
as his brawn, making the Riddler a perfect&nbsp;
match for him. He leaves behind riddles at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:40.880
every crime scene, and while they may&nbsp;
puzzle everyone else, Batman is able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:44.960
get a good handle on the situation ... for&nbsp;
the most part. This version of the Riddler&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:49.760
is unlike anything that's come before, taking&nbsp;
on the persona of a Zodiac-like serial killer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:53.920
Riddler may have killed before in the comics, but&nbsp;
unless you count most of Jim Carrey's portrayal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:58.720
he's never done anything quite this atrocious.
"Does anybody else feel like a fried egg?"&nbsp;

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:02.640
There are many gruesome scenes in "The Batman"&nbsp;
that resemble moments you might expect out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:07.200
something like "Se7en" or "Prisoners." But there's&nbsp;
one scene involving Riddler that may be tough to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:11.840
watch even for the bravest souls in the audience.
It's obvious right from the get-go that Riddler&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:15.760
doesn't have any sympathy for his victims.&nbsp;
In his mind, they are the corrupt elite,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:19.920
and they deserve what they have coming to them.&nbsp;
He's not even above sending a video of one of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:24.640
victims' demises to the local news so that&nbsp;
everyone can see his grand plan in action.&nbsp;

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:28.240
During "The Batman," we see a video&nbsp;
made by Riddler himself depicting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:32.720
his capture of Commissioner Pete Savage. The&nbsp;
commissioner isn't as clean as everyone thought,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:37.840
and Riddler highlights this fact by strapping&nbsp;
an apparatus to his body. This device contains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:43.200
various tubes linked to a rat cage, allowing&nbsp;
the rodents to burrow into him. While we don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:47.600
see anything too graphic, just the thought of&nbsp;
dying that way is a painful visual to imagine.&nbsp;

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:51.440
It's something that a lot of audiences&nbsp;
thought went too far, and if you find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:55.200
yourself having a tingle of familiarity&nbsp;
while watching the scene, you aren't alone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:59.840
The moment bears striking similarities to a scene&nbsp;
in 2008's "The Dark Knight" when the Joker sends a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:05.520
tape to the news depicting his torture of a Batman&nbsp;
impersonator. That was also a horrifying scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:09.520
particularly since it showed a darker side&nbsp;
to Joker than what most people had seen in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:13.840
a film up to that point.
"Look at me. Look at me."&nbsp;

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:17.120
From Riddler's tape to the rest of his&nbsp;
time in "The Batman," it will be hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:21.600
to look at the character the same way again.
Villains sending ominous videos to the local&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:26.000
news isn't the only thing "The Batman" and "The&nbsp;
Dark Knight" have in common. They both offer more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:30.080
grounded takes on the character, introducing us&nbsp;
to villains who could theoretically exist in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:35.600
real world. They both depict Batman wanting to go&nbsp;
after the mob and organized crime plaguing Gotham.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:39.680
And that's just the tip of the iceberg.
Both Batman movies also make excellent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:43.600
use of their funeral scenes. In "The Dark&nbsp;
Knight," there's a massive outdoor funeral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:48.160
procession for Police Commissioner Loeb. People&nbsp;
are naturally on edge from the Joker's attacks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:52.320
and as everyone should have suspected, Joker&nbsp;
targets the funeral and makes an attempt on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:57.200
mayor's life while he's at the podium. Similarly,&nbsp;
"The Batman" has a funeral scene for the fallen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:01.520
mayor. The festivities are held inside this&nbsp;
time around, but that doesn't stop Riddler&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:06.320
from sending a runaway car into the building&nbsp;
to scare the daylights out of everyone inside.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:09.920
And of course, there's a special surprise&nbsp;
waiting for everyone inside the vehicle.&nbsp;

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:16.400
"Let's clear this place out now."
Of course, these examples don't suggest that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:21.440
the two movies are anything alike. They both stand&nbsp;
alone as incredible pieces of superhero cinema,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:26.160
raising the bar for any future projects. Anything&nbsp;
they have in common is purely surface level,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:31.120
but there's honestly no way to avoid comparisons&nbsp;
between two of the best Batman movies to date.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:34.880
Both movies are brutal, and as this&nbsp;
particular Riddler scene proves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:39.680
they contain some genuinely terrifying scenes&nbsp;
that bring the Batman into darker territory.&nbsp;

